text:
{
  "can_do": "Ilen can pull fog from air at night on wet ground, violating the basic rule that weather-drawers can only work during daylight when the ground is heating the air.",
  "in_the_way": "He's stuck at the second rank out of four, which is the weakest in his cohort. The land around him has been bone-dry for three years. Whatever he can do at night, it's not enough to fix that, and probably won't be until he ranks higher—if ranking is even possible with an ability the system doesn't account for.",
  "expect_next": "Progression fantasy with a hard magic system. The coast/plain split feels deliberately engineered as a rule-break waiting to explode—he was raised in a place where the foundational assumption about how drawers work doesn't hold. I expect the book to spend time on (1) him discovering other people can't do what he does, (2) figuring out why he can, and (3) the social/political consequences of being an anomaly when there's a three-year drought and people are probably desperate.",
  "undefined_words": [],
  "open_questions": [
    "\"the farmers in the green valley below did it without meaning to\" — what did they do, and how?",
    "What breaks between the coast and the plain that makes his ability possible only there?"
  ]
}

{
  "can_do": "He can pull weather at night from wet ground, which works on a coast but shouldn't work inland—he operates under different physical rules than the system he's been placed into.",
  "in_the_way": "He's actually weak at his level on the inland plain despite his impossible ability. The region is three years into drought, and he's stuck suspecting something he can't prove.",
  "expect_next": "A magic-system fantasy about learning or fighting a system that doesn't match reality. The mystery about why the plain dried out will pull the plot, and his coastal gift will either become crucial or mark him as dangerous.",
  "undefined_words": [],
  "open_questions": [
    "Why does Ilen think the farmers in the valley below caused the drought?",
    "What happens when his ability—working weather at night where it's supposed to be impossible—becomes known?"
  ]
}

{
  "can_do": "She produces fertile offspring from grafted creatures, which shouldn't be possible—grafters are barren by definition, and breeders reject grafted flesh outright. She's somehow both.",
  "in_the_way": "She's slower at grafting than peers her age, but the real cost is political: her ability threatens the entire order that keeps breeders and grafters separate. Halder Vone brought her in to prove she's lying, but if she isn't, his family loses their standing.",
  "expect_next": "This is a hard-magic story about a protagonist who just entered a world with rules she's about to break, possibly without meaning to. I'd expect it to follow the central mystery—why she can do this—and the escalating pressure from people who need her not to.",
  "undefined_words": [
    "like flesh, then far flesh, and above those a handful of people alive who can join two things with no kinship at all"
  ],
  "open_questions": [
    "Why can she breed true when no grafter ever has?",
    "What will Halder actually do if she turns out to be telling the truth?",
    "Why did she spend four years isolated on a rooftop, and does anyone know what she was doing there?"
  ]
}

{
  "can_do": "She can join pieces of different animals together to make living hybrids—a skill most grafters have—but her grafted creatures can have offspring, which nothing a grafter makes is supposed to do.",
  "in_the_way": "A powerful breeder has brought her into his pens to either prove she's lying or, if she isn't, to destroy her. She threatens the whole system he represents.",
  "expect_next": "A hard-rule fantasy where the system matters—I expect the book to actually explain how grafting works and what it costs, not just treat it as plot furniture. Probably conflict as her ability becomes known, and hunting for why she's different.",
  "undefined_words": ["like flesh", "delta", "join"],
  "open_questions": [
    "She does not know why yet",
    "what exactly Vone does when he finds out she's not lying"
  ]
}
